# gitleaks findings that are acknowledged rather than fixable.
#
# Entries are FINGERPRINTS: commit:file:rule:line. That is the narrowest
# suppression gitleaks offers - it silences one occurrence, in one commit, in
# one file, on one line, for one rule. It cannot mask the same rule anywhere
# else, which is the point: a path- or rule-level exclusion would have turned
# the scanner off for a whole directory to settle two lines.
#
# Every entry needs the four things a suppression is worthless without: what it
# was, why it is not a risk, what was done about it, and where to look to check.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1-2. PGPASSWORD written as a literal in two CI workflows.
#
#   What:    A PGPASSWORD assignment written as a bare literal ahead of a psql
#            call, once in each of the two workflows that stand up a Postgres
#            service. (Spelled out no further here on purpose: this file is
#            scanned like any other, and quoting the offending string would
#            create a third finding to explain.)
#   Risk:    None. Both address a Postgres service container published on the
#            GitHub runner's loopback interface, created at the start of one job
#            and destroyed at the end of it. The value was never a credential to
#            anything that outlives a CI run or is reachable from outside it.
#   Fixed:   Yes, in 04af565. Both workflows now set
#            POSTGRES_HOST_AUTH_METHOD=trust, so there is no password in the
#            container to pass, and the psql calls carry no credential at all.
#            A scan of the working tree returns nothing for this rule.
#   Why here: gitleaks scans every commit in a pull request, so a literal that
#            has been removed at HEAD is still found in the commit that added
#            it. The alternative was rewriting nineteen commits of reviewed
#            history to expunge a string that was never secret.
#   Verify:  git show 04af565 -- .github/workflows/
#
c005799e9a4c0bb6d91505e5ad4106b0eaa99c34:.github/workflows/ci.yml:inline-db-password-literal:183
